**New owner rule on verifier cost (2026-10-04). It replaces rule 12's "24-48 h per setting / ≤400 GPU-h", which your Phase A draft was checked against.**

Verifying **one submission** may cost at most about **20 H100-hours per scored setting, averaged over the task's scored settings**: the task's total verifier GPU-hours for one submission ÷ the number of scored settings ≤ ~20. A run that scores several settings counts once in the total. Your own time as the building agent is still unlimited; this bounds only the verifier. The relay prompts now carry the rule (ML-Relay 99a91ab4).

**The draft is over it.** Its estimates for one submission are 8-20 (hs5-25f) + 15-45 (hs5-49f) + 15-45 (hs5-49f-vib) + 100-200 H100-h (hs5-49f-1536d: 13-25 h on 8×H100), i.e. about 140-310 H100-h over 4 settings, an average of about 35-78 per setting. The 1536d setting alone is 5-10× the per-setting budget.

**Please reply with a revised Phase A plan that fits** (needs-reply: the owner decides on it before `go`). Ways to fit, keeping the paper's protocol otherwise:
- drop or replace the 1536d setting; prefer the paper's own smaller configurations of the same model family to a new scale;
- a **step ladder** in Phase B: the shortest training length at which the baselines' ranking settles, instead of the full 200k steps;
- cheaper validation: evaluate once on the final EMA weights, with the eval-set size the score needs;
- fewer GPUs per trial where 8 scale poorly.

In the revised draft, give each setting's projected H100-hours and their average against the 20 H100-h budget, and the Phase B and Phase C totals at the new scale.

text.body:
The task currently has three settings: the target widths 256 / 1024 / 4096 of the same MLP on CIFAR-10 (μP paper, Fig. 3). They satisfy the three-setting rule, because width transfer is the research question. But they share one model family and one dataset.

Plan (owner decision 2026-10-02): keep the three MLP widths and add a fourth setting from the paper itself, μTransfer of a Transformer on WikiText-2 (Tensor Programs V, Yang et al. 2022), using the paper's own protocol: tune on the narrow proxy, transfer to the wide target, score at the target. Then:
- measure every baseline at full scale with the task's seeds;
- regenerate the score spec (geometric mean over four settings);
- re-render (no evaluation specifics in agent-visible files);
- run Gate 3;
- push the bundle here.

Maintainer-run change; it does not go through the request backend.
